    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe syntax error arises because the function firstPendingFIFO's body attempts to use a quantified expression (r: Request | ...) directly as the return value without proper parenthesis or set braces. In Alloy 6, a function body defining a set or lone value via a comprehension must be enclosed in parentheses. The colon (:) after r is not valid outside a comprehension or a set expression. The parser is expecting a closing parenthesis, indicating that the comprehension syntax is incomplete.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nEnclose the set comprehension in parentheses to form a valid Alloy 6 expression as the function body.\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: Locate the function definition for firstPendingFIFO at line 203.\n- Step 2: Replace the function body starting at line 204,\n    from:\n        (r: Request | s.reqStatus[r] = Pending and\n          no r2: Request | s.reqStatus[r2] = Pending and precedes[r2, r])\n    to:\n        (r: Request | s.reqStatus[r] = Pending and\n            no r2: Request | s.reqStatus[r2] = Pending and precedes[r2, r]\n        )\n    by wrapping the entire comprehension in parentheses:\n        ( (r: Request | s.reqStatus[r] = Pending and\n            no r2: Request | s.reqStatus[r2] = Pending and precedes[r2, r]\n          )\n        )\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nUsing parentheses to enclose the set/lone comprehension in the function body is required by Alloy 6 syntax for function definitions, and this approach avoids using curly braces or omitted enclosures that have caused similar errors in other contexts.\n\n[LESSON]:\nAlways enclose set or lone comprehensions in parentheses when using them as function bodies in Alloy 6; failing to do so leads to parsing errors due to ambiguous or incomplete comprehension syntax.\n```",
