    "evaluator_feedback": "## 1. NEXT ACTION\n\n**keep fix**\n\n- The code change (syntax correction) produced the intended result: all assertions pass, all requirements are exercised in meaningful scenarios, and no regressions or unintended behaviors are detected.\n\n---\n\n## 2. MODEL IMPROVEMENT GUIDANCE\n\nWhile the model now meets all hard metrics and covers the requirements, the following targeted improvements are recommended to further strengthen verification and avoid underspecification:\n\n### 2.1. Add Positive Test for Delegation Revocation at Emergency Exit\n\n**Issue:** Some instances may have no delegations during Emergency, so revocation logic is not always meaningfully exercised.\n\n**Action:**  \nAdd a positive test predicate to ensure at least one delegation is granted during Emergency mode, so revocation at Emergency exit is always exercised in a run:\n\n```alloy\npred TestDelegationDuringEmergency {\n  some s: State | s.mode = Emergency and some d: Delegation | d in s.activeDelegations and d.duringEmergency = True\n}\nrun TestDelegationDuringEmergency\n```\n\n### 2.2. Consider Explicitly Bounding Emergency Mode Duration\n\n**Issue:** Emergency mode is currently only required to eventually return to Normal mode, but there is no explicit upper bound on duration.\n\n**Action:**  \nIf a maximum Emergency period is required by the requirements, add a constraint or assertion to bound Emergency mode duration.  \nExample (if you wish to enforce a maximum number of consecutive Emergency states):\n\n```alloy\n// Example: Emergency must not last more than 2 transitions\nassert EmergencyMaxDuration {\n  all s: State | \n    s.mode = Emergency =>\n      some s2: State | Next[s, s2] and s2.mode = Normal\n}\ncheck EmergencyMaxDuration for 3 State\n```\n\n_Tailor the maximum according to requirement intent._\n\n### 2.3. Add Assertions for Stronger Coverage\n\n**Gap:** No assertion checks that exactly two administrators activate Emergency mode (requirement R1).\n\n**Action:**  \nAdd an assertion to enforce this behavior:\n\n```alloy\nassert assertExactlyTwoAdminsActivateEmergency {\n  all s, s2: State |\n    s.mode = Normal and s2.mode = Emergency and Next[s, s2] =>\n      one as: set Administrator | #as = 2\n}\ncheck assertExactlyTwoAdminsActivateEmergency\n```\n\n**Gap:** No assertion that admin credential updates only occur after Emergency ends.\n\n**Action:**  \nAdd:\n\n```alloy\nassert assertAdminCredUpdateOnlyAfterEmergency {\n  all s, s2: State |\n    s.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Normal and Next[s, s2] =>\n      all a: Administrator | s2.adminCredUpdated[a] = True\n  all s, s2: State |\n    not (s.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Normal and Next[s, s2]) =>\n      all a: Administrator | s2.adminCredUpdated[a] = s.adminCredUpdated[a]\n}\ncheck assertAdminCredUpdateOnlyAfterEmergency\n```\n\n### 2.4. Weaken Facts to Assertions Where Possible\n\n**Issue:** Several Emergency module properties are encoded as facts (assumed always true), which can mask errors.\n\n**Action:**  \nWhere feasible, convert core Emergency facts (e.g., `EmergencyPrivsOnlyDuringEmergency`, `EmergencyPrivsRevokedAtExit`, `DelegationRevokedAtEmergencyExit`, `DelegationRetainedAfterEmergencyIfNotDuring`) to assertions and check them. This increases the diagnostic power of your model.\n\n---\n\n## 3. ASSUMPTION REVIEW\n\n- **Emergency-mode delegation revocation facts**:  \n  **Action:** `reduce` (These could be overconstraining; consider verifying as assertions rather than enforcing as facts.)\n\n- **No explicit Emergency duration upper bound**:  \n  **Action:** `confirm` (If a strict upper bound is a requirement, clarify and encode it; otherwise, leave as is.)\n\n---\n\n## 4. REQUIREMENT UPDATE RECOMMENDATIONS\n\n- **No changes needed** to the stable requirements at this time.\n- If modeling an explicit maximum Emergency duration is required, clarify and update the requirement text accordingly.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Q&A REUSE AND UPDATE\n\n- **No prior Q&A pairs** require status updates.\n- **No unresolved interpretation questions** detected.\n- **No new questions** need to be posed to the RE agent at this stage.\n\n---\n\n## 6. STRONGER VERIFICATION CHECKS\n\n- **Recommended:**  \n  - Add the new assertions above for exact Emergency activation and credential update logic.\n  - Add negative tests (e.g., Emergency cannot be entered with fewer or more than two admins).\n  - Edge cases: Delegations pre-dating Emergency mode, no delegations during Emergency, Emergency mode not triggered.\n- **Vacuity checks:** All current assertions are non-vacuous.\n\n---\n\n## 7. PRIORITIZED HANDOFF ITEMS FOR RE\n\n**Critical (fix now):**\n- [ ] Add a positive test ensuring delegations exist during Emergency to meaningfully exercise revocation logic.\n- [ ] Add (and check) assertions for:\n    - Exactly two admins activating Emergency mode\n    - Admin credential updates only after Emergency ends\n\n**Important (next iteration):**\n- [ ] Consider weakening Emergency module facts to assertions for critical properties to expose modeling errors.\n- [ ] Confirm or clarify if a strict upper bound is needed for Emergency mode duration; add assertion if needed.\n\n**Optional/Future:**\n- [ ] Add more negative/edge-case tests (e.g., Emergency not triggered, improper admin count).\n- [ ] Continue to monitor for vacuity/underspecification as requirements evolve.\n\n---\n\n**Summary:**  \nKeep the current fix. The model is now correct and robust, but targeted enhancements will further strengthen verification against underspecification and increase diagnostic power. Implement the above refinements to converge fully.",
